<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for both apps. Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the
  hardened runtime forbids four things by default: JIT, unsigned executable memory, loading code
  signed by another team, and DYLD environment variables. A NativeAOT binary needs none of them
  (there is no JIT and no runtime-generated code), and build-dmg.sh re-signs every Mach-O it finds
  in the bundle (SDL3, MoltenVK, ONNX Runtime, the camera SDKs) with the same identity as the
  executable, so library validation passes without being switched off.

  The two below are therefore NOT granted. If a first notarization or a first launch reports
  "code signature invalid for library" for something the script did not sign (a dylib loaded from
  outside the bundle, say), the fix is to sign that library, and only failing that to enable
  disable-library-validation here. Never enable allow-jit: it is not needed and it is the one
  Apple looks at hardest.

    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key><true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key><true/>

  App Sandbox is deliberately absent: it is a Mac App Store requirement, not a notarization one, and
  the viewer's file list scans the folder of whatever file was opened, which a sandbox refuses
  (see packaging/macos/README.md).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
